issue.body:
I defined some table class in Motors plugin like this:

``` php
namespace Motors\Model\Table;

class MotorsTable extends OffersTable {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        parent::initialize($config);

        $this->hasOne('Cars', ['foreignKey' => 'offer_id']);
    }
```

and I define Cars table:

``` php
namespace Motors\Model\Table;

class CarsTable extends AppTable {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        $this->table('motors_cars');
        parent::initialize($config);
    }
```

And in some controller I load:

``` php
$motors = TableRegistry::get('Motors.Motors')->find(...)->contain(['Cars'])->first();
```

While trying to load association I get error: "Cannot describe cars. It has 0 columns.". I know that error - it means MotorsTable has association to Cars as Cake\ORM\Table instead of Motors\Model\Table\CarsTable and EagerLoader doesn't know right db table.
But, if I modify MotorsTable like this:

``` php
namespace Motors\Model\Table;

class MotorsTable extends OffersTable {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        parent::initialize($config);

        // point relation to get right table class
        $this->hasOne('Cars', ['foreignKey' => 'offer_id',
            'className' => 'Motors\Model\Table\CarsTable']);

        // OR:

        // load right class table into registry first
        TableRegistry::get('Motors.Cars');
        // and EagerLoader can get right table by internal calling
        // TableRegistry::get('Cars') - returns Motors\Model\Table\CarsTable
        $this->hasOne('Cars', ['foreignKey' => 'offer_id']);
    }
```

In both cases right Cars table class is now properly loaded.

Now, maybe Cake should accept relation like this:
`$this->hasOne('Motors.Cars')` - and inside Association class we split this name to plugin and real name (to use in other places in application), but eager loader may use full name with plugin?

This is a (multiple allowed):
- [x] bug
- [ ] enhancement
- [ ] feature-discussion (RFC)
- CakePHP Version: 2.8.5
- Platform and Target: N/A
### What you did

To isolate this issue, I installed a fresh cakephp 2.8 via composer, baked a skeleton project, then added in two plugins:

`Cat` & `Dog`

I gave each plugin a `Model` folder, gave them both their own `CatAppModel`/`DogAppModel` and then gave them both a `Pet` model which looked like this:

Cat:

```
<?php

class Pet extends CatAppModel {

    public function sound() {
        return 'meow';
    }
}

```

Dog:

```
<?php

class Pet extends DogAppModel {

    public function sound() {
        return 'woof';
    }
}

```

Then I did the following in the `PagesController`:

```
        debug('Starting test, dumping map');
        debug(ClassRegistry::keys());
        debug('Loading the cat model and meowing');
        debug(ClassRegistry::init('Cat.pet')->sound());
        debug('dumping the keys');
        debug(ClassRegistry::keys());
        debug('Removing Cat model');
//sidenote, I'm aware of the bug here, so I'm unloading what the bug loads
        ClassRegistry::removeObject('pet'); 
        debug(ClassRegistry::keys());
        debug('Loading the dog model and woofing');
        debug(ClassRegistry::init('Dog.pet')->sound());
        debug('dumping the keys');
        debug(ClassRegistry::keys());
        debug('Removing Dog model');
        ClassRegistry::removeObject('pet');
        debug(ClassRegistry::keys());
```
### Expected Behavior

I would expect the following output:

```
'Starting test, dumping map'
array()
'Loading the cat model and meowing'
'meow'
'dumping the keys'
array(
    (int) 0 => 'cat_pet'
)
'Removing Cat model'
array()
'Loading the dog model and woofing'
'woof'
'dumping the keys'
array(
    (int) 0 => 'dog_pet'
)
'Removing Dog model'
array()
```
### Actual Behavior

```
'Starting test, dumping map'
array()
'Loading the cat model and meowing'
'meow'
'dumping the keys'
array(
    (int) 0 => 'pet'
)
'Removing Cat model'
array()
'Loading the dog model and woofing'
'meow'
'dumping the keys'
array(
    (int) 0 => 'pet'
)
'Removing Dog model'
array()
```

I even tried it with aliases, using these two lines to instanciate Cat.pet and Dog.pet:

`debug(ClassRegistry::init(['class'=> 'Cat.pet', 'alias' => 'cat_pet'])->sound());`

`debug(ClassRegistry::init(['class' => 'Dog.pet', 'alias' => 'dog_pet'])->sound());`

(and call `cat_pet`/`dog_pet` in the `removeObject` calls)

The output isn't any different from the above.

The expectation given by ClassRegistry::init accepting plugin syntax is that it honours it and loads the one you logically expect. In this case it's loading the first one it can find, alphabetically, regardless of how you try to specify it.
